# Cross-origin isolation for the wasm engine (SharedArrayBuffer).
#
# These apply to static assets only — Cloudflare Pages does not apply `_headers` to Pages
# Function responses, so `functions/s/[[shareId]].ts` sets both itself. Changing them here
# means changing them there too.
#
# DO NOT add a `404.html` to this directory. Pages serves it instead of `index.html` for
# unmatched paths, which is the SPA fallback every share link depends on: `/s/<id>` calls
# `context.next()` expecting `index.html`, and would render the error page instead. The
# post-deploy smoke test (scripts/share-smoke.mjs) asserts against exactly this.
#
# The `/*` rule also covers `/sw.js`, which is what makes the service worker legal to
# register on a `require-corp` page at all. Narrowing it to `/assets/*` would break the
# worker as well as the page. `scripts/webkit-pwa-check.mjs` reads this file rather than
# hardcoding the two headers, so removing them fails that check instead of shipping.

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp

# Belt and braces on the one file whose staleness has no recovery path: a browser that
# holds an old `sw.js` keeps serving from it, and the fix would be asking people to clear
# site data — which for an installed app means deleting it. Registration's default
# `updateViaCache: 'imports'` already bypasses the HTTP cache for the top-level worker
# script, and Pages already serves unhashed files `max-age=0`; this only means neither has
# to stay true for the recovery in `src/pwa/swKill.ts` to reach anybody.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/sw-kill.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
